## 00884 PATIENT=1 DOCTOR=2
1: I can't concentrate on anything lately. I think I might have ADHD, because I've always been a bit like this. ~ cc s1
2: Thanks for telling me. Let's talk it through. What happens when you try to concentrate? ~ s1
1: My mind just wanders off. I start something and then I forget what I was doing. ~ s1
2: How long has this been a problem? ~ s1
1: Since school, really. But it's worse now that I'm working from home. ~ s1
2: Is it only with work, or everywhere? ~
1: Everywhere. Reading, films, even conversations. I zone out. ~
2: How are you sleeping? ~ s2
1: Badly. I lie awake until two or three in the morning. ~ s2
2: Any low mood or worry that could be contributing? ~ n1
1: No, I'm not low. I'm not particularly anxious either. ~ n1
2: Are you taking any medicines at the moment? ~ m1
1: Just a multivitamin. And occasionally a sleeping tablet, I think it's zolpidem, 5 mg, but I'm not sure of the dose. ~ m1 m2 u1
2: How often do you take the sleeping tablet? ~ m2
1: Maybe once a week. ~ m2
2: Thank you. Do you have any allergies to medicines? ~ a1
1: When I was a child I reacted badly to penicillin and I've avoided it since. ~ a1
2: Noted. Do you drink coffee or alcohol? ~ so1
1: Coffee, yes. Six or seven cups a day. ~ so1
2: That's a lot, and it can disturb sleep and concentration. Let me check your pulse and pressure. ~
1: Okay. ~ pause=10
2: Pulse 78, blood pressure 122 over 76, temperature 36.7, oxygen 99 percent, breathing 14. ~ v
1: Normal? ~
2: Normal. Let me ask a few more things about school. Did your teachers ever comment on your attention? ~ h1
1: Yes. My reports always said I was easily distracted and daydreaming. ~ h1
2: That history matters, because ADHD has to start in childhood. ~ d1
1: So could it be ADHD? ~
2: It's possible, but poor sleep and six or seven coffees can look very much the same. I can't tell yet. ~
1: What next? ~
2: Cut the coffee to two cups, and aim for a regular sleep time. We'll see how much changes. ~ c1
1: I can try. ~
2: If it doesn't help, I'd refer you for a specialist assessment, rather than start any medicine today. ~ c2
1: All right. Thank you. ~
